== Description ==

Objects To Python generates a Python script from objects in the active document. It exports the selected objects, or all document objects when the selection is empty. The generated code creates objects and sets their properties, with additional handling for sketches, spreadsheets and PartDesign bodies.

The code appears in an editable dialog, where it can be copied, saved or executed in a new document.

== Installation ==

Install '''Objects To Python''' from the macros list in the [[Std_AddonMgr|Addon Manager]]. The macro has supporting files, including {{FileName|ObjectsToPython/ObjectsToPython.ui}}; keep these files in the locations provided by the installation.

The current macro requires the {{incode|PyQt6}} Python module in the FreeCAD environment.

== Usage ==

# Open the document containing the objects to export and make it the active document.
# Select the objects to export in the [[Tree_view|Tree view]], or clear the selection to export all objects. Selecting a group or an object with linked source objects also includes those related objects.
# Run {{FileName|ObjectsToPython.FCMacro}}. The '''Objects To Python''' dialog opens and immediately displays generated code.
# Copy the code from the editor, or choose {{Button|Save to file...}} to save its current contents. The save dialog provides a Python files filter; use a {{FileName|.py}} filename for a Python script.

The dialog provides these controls:

* {{Button|Generate code}}: generate code from the current active document and selection, appending it to the editor. Use {{Button|Clear}} first to replace an earlier export.
* {{Button|Clear}}: empty the editor.
* {{Button|Save to file...}}: save the current editor contents as a UTF-8 text file.
* {{Button|Execute}}: open a new document named {{incode|PythonToObjects}} and execute the current editor contents. To export from the original document again, make that document active before generating more code.
* {{Button|Close}}: close the dialog.

== Supported objects ==

Export support is incomplete for some object types. In particular, the macro's author notes that objects implemented by Python scripts, such as Draft objects, do not export correctly.

